  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periodic, isolated to scattered rain showers \nand thunderstorms expected through Friday night. \n\nCyclonic W'erly to NW'erly flow aloft and embedded shortwave\ntroughs impact our region through Friday night. At the surface,\na secondary cold front should sweep SE'ward through Western and\nCentral NY this evening through about daybreak Friday morning.\nThis front will be preceded by a rather humid air mass \noriginating over the Gulf. Behind the front, a high pressure \nridge associated with a cooler and less humid air mass will \nbuild from northern ON and vicinity through Friday night. Highs \nwill reach the lower 70's to lower 80's late this afternoon and \nbe followed by overnight lows in mainly the mid 50's to mid 60's\naround daybreak Friday. Friday will feature late afternoon \nhighs in the 70's. Overnight lows should reach mainly the upper \n40's to upper 50's around daybreak Saturday. Partial clearing, \nlingering low-level moisture, and weak surface winds should \nyield localized steam fog in the river and lake valleys east of \nLake Ontario, in the Finger Lakes, and in the Western Southern \nTier around daybreak Friday and Saturday, respectively.\n\nPeriodic, isolated to scattered, and organized showers and \nthunderstorms are expected late this afternoon through Friday \nevening, especially from the Niagara Frontier to northern Cayuga\nCounty and points south (greatest potential from southern Erie\nCounty to southwestern Ontario County and farther south), as \ndeep-layer bulk shear remains moderate to strong and weak to \nmoderate CAPE, including elevated CAPE, is released by the \nfollowing: moist isentropic ascent ahead of shortwave trough \naxes; low-level convergence/moist ascent along the secondary \ncold front and surface trough axes attendant to the shortwave \ntroughs; downshear outflow boundaries interacting favorably with\nambient low-level vertical wind shear. While the severe \nconvective weather risk is much greater in NE OH and NW PA, \ncannot rule-out a few severe thunderstorms impacting southern \nportions of Chautauqua and Cattaraugus Counties late this \nafternoon into this evening. If severe storms occur, damaging \nstraight-line wind gusts are expected to be the main hazard due \nto the aforementioned instability and deep-layer bulk shear \ncombined with the following: moderate DCAPE due to diurnal \nconvective mixing of the boundary layer and steepening low-level\nlapse rates through this late afternoon and then gradual \nboundary layer stabilization/reduction of DCAPE this evening as \nnocturnal cooling occurs at a somewhat slow rate in the \nwarm/moist sector. Periods of heavy rain are expected, \nespecially with showers and storms in the warm sector, due to \nthe aforementioned rich low-level moisture originating over the \nGulf. Localized flash flooding cannot be ruled-out where \nmultiple rounds of heavy rain-producing showers/storms manage to\noccur. Fair weather is expected CWA-wide after midnight Friday \nnight as the aforementioned surface ridge continues to build \ninto our region, the ridge aloft begins to build from the Upper \nMidwest, and stabilizing subsidence becomes more extensive and \nstrengthens over Western and Central NY.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Quieter weather expected this Labor Day weekend.\n\nCurrent odds favor fair weather this Saturday through Labor Day\nas the surface ridge settles S'ward and over our region and the\nridge aloft continues to build from the Upper Midwest. However,\nisolated rain showers may impact the Eastern Lake Ontario \nRegion at times on Saturday and Sunday as shortwave troughs \nembedded in the NW'erly flow aloft impact that part of our CWA. \nNear-average to slightly below-average daily high and low \ntemperatures are expected through Labor Day as the aforementioned\nridge is accompanied by a cooler and drier air mass. Mainly \nclear sky and weak or calm surface winds should yield steam fog \nin the river and lake valleys east of Lake Ontario, in the \nFinger Lakes, and in the Western Southern Tier around daybreak \neach day.",
